1
00:00:52,804 --> 00:00:54,703
DRIVER: How˙s that?
DIANA: Yep. Thank you.

2
00:00:54,704 --> 00:00:56,795
What time do you leave?

3
00:00:57,474 --> 00:00:59,747
Oh, look, it˙s hopscotch.

4
00:01:01,214 --> 00:01:02,343
Let˙s have a go.

5
00:01:02,344 --> 00:01:04,813
What are you supposed to do?
Not tread on the lines?

6
00:01:04,814 --> 00:01:06,304
Youl

7
00:01:10,554 --> 00:01:12,083
Are you going to have an operation?

8
00:01:12,084 --> 00:01:15,523
No. Today they˙re just going to measure
where to put the pellet.

9
00:01:15,524 --> 00:01:19,563
- Will it hurt when they do it?
- No. The brain doesn˙t feel anything.

10
00:01:19,564 --> 00:01:20,928
Why doesn˙t it?

11
00:01:21,764 --> 00:01:26,533
Because nerve endings feel pain
and the brain doesn˙t have any.

12
00:01:26,534 --> 00:01:28,716
Hey, Di. Hello, Michael.

13
00:01:28,904 --> 00:01:30,673
Go through to the office?

14
00:01:30,674 --> 00:01:33,273
- Is something wrong?
- Come to the office.

15
00:01:33,274 --> 00:01:34,843
What˙s wrong?
Has something happened to me?

16
00:01:34,844 --> 00:01:36,483
Well, it˙s not you. It˙s not really.

17
00:01:36,484 --> 00:01:39,883
Armac, my treatment˙s supposed to start.

18
00:01:39,884 --> 00:01:44,453
- What˙s happening?
- I˙m sorry. It˙s all to do with money.

19
00:01:44,454 --> 00:01:48,545
- The Trust can˙t afford to treat you.
- What?

20
00:01:48,694 --> 00:01:50,193
Can they do that this late on?

21
00:01:50,194 --> 00:01:52,933
This treatment˙s
part of a research trial.

22
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:59,025
They don˙t have to pay for it.
I˙m sorry, Di. I did argue your case.

23
00:02:03,374 --> 00:02:06,124
- Mrs Wrekin.
- Marge.

24
00:02:08,274 --> 00:02:11,543
- What does he say, Marge? What?
- Shh.

25
00:02:11,544 --> 00:02:13,272
Marge, is he there?

26
00:02:13,454 --> 00:02:15,883
- Will you be quiet, Jackson?
- I will not.

27
00:02:15,884 --> 00:02:20,430
Will you both address the court
and not each other?

28
00:02:22,594 --> 00:02:23,794
Mrs Wrekin.

29
00:02:25,564 --> 00:02:30,633
Would you mind telling the court
exactly what it is you have there?

30
00:02:30,634 --> 00:02:32,544
It˙s my son, Dominic.

31
00:02:33,234 --> 00:02:36,234
- The angel.
- In a cremation urn?

32
00:02:36,804 --> 00:02:39,532
He˙s communicating his wishes.

33
00:02:40,614 --> 00:02:44,433
It˙s your wishes
the court has to consider.

34
00:02:47,084 --> 00:02:50,883
The lower court imposed an order
on your husband

35
00:02:50,884 --> 00:02:53,723
to stay downstairs and not come up
to your part of the house.

36
00:02:53,724 --> 00:02:56,123
He has broken this order
on several occasions.

37
00:02:56,124 --> 00:02:58,323
Well, the blessed Dominic
told him he could.

38
00:02:58,324 --> 00:02:59,464
He did so.

39
00:02:59,934 --> 00:03:02,733
And did he also say that it was all
right for your husband to assault you

40
00:03:02,734 --> 00:03:05,825
- on those occasions?
- He did notl

41
00:03:07,074 --> 00:03:10,173
- He did forgive him, though.
- He˙s the angel himself.

42
00:03:10,174 --> 00:03:13,043
Well, Mr Wrekin, you˙ve broken
this order on more than one occasion.

43
00:03:13,044 --> 00:03:16,013
- Didn˙t the blessed Dominic say so?
- He did right enough.

44
00:03:16,014 --> 00:03:19,013
Unfortunately, he doesn˙t have
right of audience here.

45
00:03:19,014 --> 00:03:20,924
He speaks through me.

46
00:03:23,424 --> 00:03:24,594
Well, then,

47
00:03:25,154 --> 00:03:32,064
would you like to ask him what he thinks
about my lifting the order entirely?

48
00:03:37,104 --> 00:03:38,104
Oh.

49
00:03:39,404 --> 00:03:43,041
- He˙s not very keen on that.
- He˙s not?

50
00:03:43,474 --> 00:03:46,843
Well, he˙s gonna have to live with it
because that˙s what I˙m going to do.

51
00:03:46,844 --> 00:03:48,473
I think you deserve each other

52
00:03:48,474 --> 00:03:52,838
and shouldn˙t waste
any more of the court˙s time.

53
00:03:53,454 --> 00:03:54,564
All rise.

54
00:03:57,954 --> 00:04:01,553
You have the nurse, Diana Hulsey,
back with the mobile phone case.

55
00:04:01,554 --> 00:04:03,463
Oh, good. About time.

56
00:04:03,464 --> 00:04:06,010
- One-Way are settling.
- Oh.

57
00:04:06,594 --> 00:04:11,958
- Do we know for how much?
- No, not yet. I˙ll get your tea.

58
00:04:15,374 --> 00:04:16,829
(GAVEL POUNDING)

59
00:04:17,004 --> 00:04:18,459
USHER: All rise.

60
00:04:30,524 --> 00:04:32,153
I˙m pleased to inform the court

61
00:04:32,154 --> 00:04:35,153
the claimant and defendants
have reached a settlement.

62
00:04:35,154 --> 00:04:36,234
I˙m glad.

63
00:04:36,494 --> 00:04:40,333
Whatever the reasons,
such actions are often stressful.

64
00:04:40,334 --> 00:04:42,563
And expensive. Mrs Mills.

65
00:04:42,564 --> 00:04:46,603
The instructing solicitor hasn˙t yet
confirmed the claimant˙s acceptance.

66
00:04:46,604 --> 00:04:49,473
Well, is there reason to believe that
Ms Hulsey has changed her mind?

67
00:04:49,474 --> 00:04:53,073
It is unusual for her not to be in touch
or be here.

68
00:04:53,074 --> 00:04:54,620
Perhaps she died.

69
00:04:54,714 --> 00:04:59,624
- Might a delay be helpful?
- I˙m sure she hasn˙t died.

70
00:05:00,054 --> 00:05:02,873
My Lord, might I have a moment?

71
00:05:14,194 --> 00:05:15,793
Is it too late to change my mind?

72
00:05:15,794 --> 00:05:17,833
- Why? Has something happened?
- Yes.

73
00:05:17,834 --> 00:05:21,473
How did you know the Hospital Trust
are blocking Diana˙s treatment?

74
00:05:21,474 --> 00:05:24,943
- I questioned the staff.
- Is it an official investigation?

75
00:05:24,944 --> 00:05:27,854
Hardly. I˙m still on sick leave.

76
00:05:29,074 --> 00:05:31,383
But being a police sergeant,
they˙ll talk.

77
00:05:31,384 --> 00:05:33,253
Will you tell the judge?

78
00:05:33,254 --> 00:05:36,253
If I do, he might not
continue the hearing.

79
00:05:36,254 --> 00:05:40,593
- I want to go through with this.
- Despite what the stress will do?

80
00:05:40,594 --> 00:05:43,140
I want to do it for him, Jo.

81
00:05:44,124 --> 00:05:45,793
Charlie say what she wanted?

82
00:05:45,794 --> 00:05:49,976
Sometimes girls just like
to talk to their dad.

83
00:05:54,874 --> 00:05:57,143
- CHARLIE: Hello.
- Charlie, hi.

84
00:05:57,144 --> 00:05:58,690
- Get off!
- What?

85
00:05:59,144 --> 00:06:01,713
It˙s all right. I˙m talking to the dog.

86
00:06:01,714 --> 00:06:04,624
- How˙s work?
- There'salotof it.

87
00:06:04,644 --> 00:06:08,883
Yeah. Final years are like that.
Am I going to see you?

88
00:06:08,884 --> 00:06:11,623
I don˙t know.
I˙m on the roof doing a sit-in.

89
00:06:11,624 --> 00:06:13,114
Arefinalssohard?

90
00:06:13,754 --> 00:06:16,353
The college wants
to put up a phone mast.

91
00:06:16,354 --> 00:06:20,264
- Canyouhelp?
- Oh, my sit-in days are over.

92
00:06:20,534 --> 00:06:23,733
- I˙d like to pick your brain.
- All right. We could have dinner.

93
00:06:23,734 --> 00:06:29,007
- When do you get off the roof?
- Okay, take it away, King.

94
00:06:30,774 --> 00:06:32,603
Up until a short while ago,

95
00:06:32,604 --> 00:06:36,443
the defendants were under the impression
the claimant had accepted their offer.

96
00:06:36,444 --> 00:06:40,613
She˙s now changed her mind.
In case she changes it yet again,

97
00:06:40,614 --> 00:06:46,160
thus saving time, money and
unnecessary distress to my client,

98
00:06:47,324 --> 00:06:50,723
the defendant˙s offer will
remain on the table.

99
00:06:50,724 --> 00:06:52,906
Thank you, Mrs Channing.

100
00:06:53,734 --> 00:06:55,993
Are we ready to select a jury?

101
00:06:55,994 --> 00:06:58,703
My Lord, as I indicated before,

102
00:06:58,704 --> 00:07:01,833
I hardly think this is a case
for a jury.

103
00:07:01,834 --> 00:07:05,603
This class of action is never tried
by a jury nowadays.

104
00:07:05,604 --> 00:07:07,514
It˙s too complicated.

105
00:07:07,674 --> 00:07:10,843
You underestimate juries, Mrs Channing.

106
00:07:10,844 --> 00:07:16,513
In the past, juries always decided
this class of action, as you put it.

107
00:07:16,514 --> 00:07:19,353
If ever there was a case
that should go before a jury,

108
00:07:19,354 --> 00:07:23,893
it˙s whether Diana Hulsey˙s
mobile phone caused her brain tumour.

109
00:07:23,894 --> 00:07:25,993
In any event, under Section 69,

110
00:07:25,994 --> 00:07:29,533
Subsection 3,
of the Supreme Court Act, 1981,

111
00:07:29,534 --> 00:07:33,233
the jury trial is something
I decide should happen.

112
00:07:33,234 --> 00:07:36,873
An emotionally reactive jury
greatly disadvantages my client.

113
00:07:36,874 --> 00:07:40,511
Judges have emotions, too, Mrs Channing.

114
00:07:40,674 --> 00:07:46,613
We˙re just more skilled at hiding them.
Let us proceed to jury selection.

115
00:07:46,614 --> 00:07:49,283
I˙d like you to consider, if you will,

116
00:07:49,284 --> 00:07:54,693
what single item in modern living
has come to represent freedom,

117
00:07:54,694 --> 00:07:58,474
opportunity and, above all, convenience.

118
00:08:01,194 --> 00:08:03,933
I think you˙ll agree
it˙s the mobile phone.

119
00:08:03,934 --> 00:08:08,373
For Diana Hulsey, a busy,
post-operative cancer counsellor,

120
00:08:08,374 --> 00:08:12,473
with her own life
and that of her young son to organise,

121
00:08:12,474 --> 00:08:15,293
her mobile phone was essential.

122
00:08:15,674 --> 00:08:20,653
To her, it was both the symbol
and the instrument of freedom.

123
00:08:20,654 --> 00:08:25,291
But it was to become
a dangerous, obsessive shackle.

124
00:08:27,154 --> 00:08:29,494
This little instrument

125
00:08:30,894 --> 00:08:34,333
would not only burn into the brain
of this young mother,

126
00:08:34,334 --> 00:08:37,698
causing a massive, inoperable tumour,

127
00:08:38,004 --> 00:08:43,368
it would do so with the fore-knowledge
of the manufacturers.

128
00:08:44,844 --> 00:08:47,473
And why does my learned friend
think he deserves it?

129
00:08:47,474 --> 00:08:50,384
- ˙Cause it˙s time for tea.
- Hi.

130
00:08:51,014 --> 00:08:54,283
- I˙m gonna be a barrister, Mum.
- Oh, good.

131
00:08:54,284 --> 00:08:58,557
He put the case for a cream tea
very succinctly.

132
00:09:07,334 --> 00:09:09,880
This way, my learned friend.

133
00:09:23,984 --> 00:09:25,034
Rachel.

134
00:09:25,484 --> 00:09:29,083
I˙m impressed with the performance
of my rivals.

135
00:09:29,084 --> 00:09:32,683
Oh, you mean Mrs Mills and Mrs Channing.

136
00:09:32,684 --> 00:09:37,048
Don˙t your personal relationships
cause problems?

137
00:09:37,864 --> 00:09:40,793
There˙s a rule about who a judge
can have in front of him.

138
00:09:40,794 --> 00:09:43,293
Ex-wives, ex-lovers, no problem.

139
00:09:43,294 --> 00:09:46,204
Sons and daughters, a bit dodgy.

140
00:09:46,464 --> 00:09:51,803
With current wives and current lovers,
that˙s a definite no-no.

141
00:09:51,804 --> 00:09:55,073
There˙s a risk that pillow talk
might give an unfair advantage.

142
00:09:55,074 --> 00:09:57,529
You would never allow that.

143
00:09:58,084 --> 00:09:59,721
No. I suppose not.

144
00:10:02,714 --> 00:10:04,983
I told you when this started,
I wouldn˙t wait by the phone.

145
00:10:04,984 --> 00:10:05,984
Yes.

146
00:10:08,024 --> 00:10:11,063
- I have been rather busy.
- Is that an excuse not to call?

147
00:10:11,064 --> 00:10:14,193
No. I have no excuse for my discourtesy.

148
00:10:14,194 --> 00:10:18,333
I thought I˙d see
when the sign got turned off.

149
00:10:18,334 --> 00:10:21,203
I didn˙t think it would happen so fast.

150
00:10:21,204 --> 00:10:24,841
- You can call me.
- But will you answer?

151
00:10:27,974 --> 00:10:31,611
Perhaps I should have stayed in therapy.

152
00:10:31,644 --> 00:10:36,313
- Come on. Let˙s get some tea.
- You were never in therapy, John.

153
00:10:36,314 --> 00:10:41,314
You˙re too intellectually proud
to accept you need help.

154
00:10:52,464 --> 00:10:55,533
They˙re staying up there all night.

155
00:10:55,534 --> 00:10:58,743
We˙re trying to keep someone up there
the whole time.

156
00:10:58,744 --> 00:11:02,343
Is that all you˙ve got?
How many students are involved?

157
00:11:02,344 --> 00:11:05,708
Not enough. This isn˙t the ˙60s, Dad.

158
00:11:07,554 --> 00:11:12,623
- And what˙s the college say?
- Masts don˙t cause health problems.

159
00:11:12,624 --> 00:11:13,923
Do they know that?

160
00:11:13,924 --> 00:11:18,823
The government line. The Stewart Report.
"Mobile Phones and Health."

161
00:11:18,824 --> 00:11:19,964
Ms Hulsey,

162
00:11:21,534 --> 00:11:26,003
can you tell the court why you˙ve
been attending hospital recently?

163
00:11:26,004 --> 00:11:30,303
To check the progress of an astrocytoma
of the left parietal lobe.

164
00:11:30,304 --> 00:11:35,443
- Which is what, exactly?
- It˙s a malignant brain tumour.

165
00:11:35,444 --> 00:11:36,943
Is this operable?

166
00:11:36,944 --> 00:11:40,153
No. If they operate, it would destroy
too much brain tissue.

167
00:11:40,154 --> 00:11:42,183
Is there alternative treatment?

168
00:11:42,184 --> 00:11:45,283
There˙s an implant
the doctors were going to try.

169
00:11:45,284 --> 00:11:48,593
It meant putting radioactive
cobalt pellets near the tumour.

170
00:11:48,594 --> 00:11:51,293
- It was abandoned.
- Do you know why?

171
00:11:51,294 --> 00:11:54,385
Armac... Dr Colbin, my consultant,

172
00:11:54,634 --> 00:11:57,063
said that the Hospital Trust
couldn˙t afford it.

173
00:11:57,064 --> 00:11:58,933
They can refuse treatment

174
00:11:58,934 --> 00:12:01,933
because it is experimental and
there˙s no guarantee it will succeed.

175
00:12:01,934 --> 00:12:04,903
Then why was the treatment ever mooted?

176
00:12:04,904 --> 00:12:06,913
Dr Colbin said it was my only option.

177
00:12:06,914 --> 00:12:08,013
(STAMMERING) No...

178
00:12:08,014 --> 00:12:11,994
I˙m confused.
Did they start the treatment?

179
00:12:12,614 --> 00:12:15,783
I did the test
and they accepted me on the programme.

180
00:12:15,784 --> 00:12:18,383
I signed the papers accepting the risk.

181
00:12:18,384 --> 00:12:20,823
I was then told
that it wouldn˙t go ahead.

182
00:12:20,824 --> 00:12:24,163
Were the doctors being callous,
raising false hopes?

183
00:12:24,164 --> 00:12:29,892
No. They were very nice.
It was the Hospital Trust that said no.

184
00:12:29,894 --> 00:12:31,903
They˙d been approached
by the defendant˙s lawyers.

185
00:12:31,904 --> 00:12:33,703
My Lord, I must protest.

186
00:12:33,704 --> 00:12:37,603
We˙ve patiently sat through
less than relevant information

187
00:12:37,604 --> 00:12:41,413
about a second misfortune
to have befallen the claimant.

188
00:12:41,414 --> 00:12:45,983
Now we hear the most scandalous
of prejudicial statements engineered

189
00:12:45,984 --> 00:12:47,253
by my learned friend.

190
00:12:47,254 --> 00:12:53,164
I am concerned that the jury heard this.
Would you go out, please?

191
00:13:01,164 --> 00:13:04,863
Mrs Mills, is there evidence
to support this claim?

192
00:13:04,864 --> 00:13:07,773
Enquiries on behalf of the claimant
show an approach was made

193
00:13:07,774 --> 00:13:09,273
by the defendant˙s lawyers.

194
00:13:09,274 --> 00:13:11,973
Are you calling any witnesses
to support this?

195
00:13:11,974 --> 00:13:14,113
Yes. I˙m calling Ms Hulsey˙s consultant

196
00:13:14,114 --> 00:13:17,183
who was told by the Hospital Trust
not to treat her.

197
00:13:17,184 --> 00:13:19,413
Then I will permit
the line of questioning.

198
00:13:19,414 --> 00:13:24,253
I fail to see the relevance of the
furrow my learned friend is ploughing.

199
00:13:24,254 --> 00:13:28,123
That we shall have to wait
to discover, Mrs Channing.

200
00:13:28,124 --> 00:13:30,397
Let˙s have the jury back.

201
00:13:30,564 --> 00:13:32,293
JO: Ms Hulsey, to what end

202
00:13:32,294 --> 00:13:35,733
did the defendant˙s lawyers
approach the Hospital Trust?

203
00:13:35,734 --> 00:13:38,663
To withdraw treatment
so as to put pressure on me.

204
00:13:38,664 --> 00:13:40,163
No, My Lord. This is not acceptable.

205
00:13:40,164 --> 00:13:45,873
No. It˙s unpleasant, Mrs Channing. You
can challenge it in cross-examination.

206
00:13:45,874 --> 00:13:48,043
How did you interpret that pressure?

207
00:13:48,044 --> 00:13:49,873
They wanted me to accept the offer
and settle.

208
00:13:49,874 --> 00:13:52,124
I fail to see how.

209
00:13:53,014 --> 00:13:54,304
As we˙re here.

210
00:13:54,614 --> 00:13:59,123
I would save it for cross-examination,
Mrs Channing.

211
00:13:59,124 --> 00:14:01,623
However, counsel for the defendant
does have a point, Mrs Mills.

212
00:14:01,624 --> 00:14:03,352
You should move on.

213
00:14:06,394 --> 00:14:09,663
Ms Hulsey, when was your condition
first diagnosed?

214
00:14:09,664 --> 00:14:10,774
It was...

215
00:14:15,634 --> 00:14:16,804
Ms Hulsey?

216
00:14:18,604 --> 00:14:19,624
Diana?

217
00:14:26,044 --> 00:14:29,226
Would you like a short adjournment?

218
00:14:34,094 --> 00:14:35,640
Can you continue?

219
00:14:40,934 --> 00:14:45,116
Perhaps you˙d be more comfortable
sitting down.

220
00:14:45,804 --> 00:14:47,986
No. I˙m fine, thank you.

221
00:14:52,144 --> 00:14:54,872
When did you first become ill?

222
00:14:56,814 --> 00:14:59,269
It was about two years ago.

223
00:15:00,354 --> 00:15:04,445
Michael had just had
his sixth birthday party.

224
00:15:04,584 --> 00:15:07,584
His school friend had slept over.

225
00:15:08,024 --> 00:15:11,363
I got a headache which didn˙t go away.

226
00:15:11,364 --> 00:15:15,393
I took some paracetamol.
It shifted for a while.

227
00:15:15,394 --> 00:15:19,463
But it kept coming back,
always on the left side of my head.

228
00:15:19,464 --> 00:15:22,133
Did you do anything about the headaches?

229
00:15:22,134 --> 00:15:24,862
Well, I took more painkillers.

230
00:15:25,444 --> 00:15:28,172
I tried to not think about it.

231
00:15:30,384 --> 00:15:32,283
But then I noticed
that my left ear got hot

232
00:15:32,284 --> 00:15:35,739
whenever I used my mobile phone a lot.

233
00:15:36,114 --> 00:15:41,024
And then I˙d get this burning sensation
when I used it.

234
00:15:41,854 --> 00:15:43,673
Like a dull earache.

235
00:15:44,264 --> 00:15:49,293
- Not painful but uncomfortable.
- What sort of phone were you using?

236
00:15:49,294 --> 00:15:50,294
ZP-9.

237
00:15:53,774 --> 00:15:56,333
- Is this your phone, Ms Hulsey?
- Yes. That˙s my phone.

238
00:15:56,334 --> 00:16:00,543
- Do you know who makes these phones?
- Yes. One-Way.

239
00:16:00,544 --> 00:16:05,272
Of all the phones on the market,
why choose this one?

240
00:16:05,414 --> 00:16:09,233
Because I liked the look of it.
It˙s small.

241
00:16:09,814 --> 00:16:12,996
It offered a package of free calls.

242
00:16:13,224 --> 00:16:15,453
Well, the ZP-9 was a cheap upgrade.

243
00:16:15,454 --> 00:16:18,923
When did you get this cheap upgrade
and package?

244
00:16:18,924 --> 00:16:20,924
Three Christmases ago.

245
00:16:21,634 --> 00:16:24,703
Michael and I were doing last-minute
shopping on Christmas Eve.

246
00:16:24,704 --> 00:16:27,523
How often do you use the phone?

247
00:16:27,934 --> 00:16:31,207
Not often. About 10, 12 times a day.

248
00:16:31,244 --> 00:16:32,773
According to your phone records...

249
00:16:32,774 --> 00:16:35,113
They˙re marked 4A
in your bundle, My Lord.

250
00:16:35,114 --> 00:16:39,643
It was sometimes 30 calls a day
at 1 minute 15 seconds.

251
00:16:39,644 --> 00:16:43,554
Your average call rate
was 23 minutes a day.

252
00:16:44,424 --> 00:16:46,879
Did you receive many calls?

253
00:16:47,194 --> 00:16:50,053
Well, the hospital called me
when I was with patients.

254
00:16:50,054 --> 00:16:54,418
Patients called me.
Parents of Michael˙s friends.

255
00:16:55,934 --> 00:16:58,603
Did you read
the ZP-9˙s instruction manual?

256
00:16:58,604 --> 00:16:59,604
I think so.

257
00:16:59,804 --> 00:17:03,573
Did you read a warning
against using this phone

258
00:17:03,574 --> 00:17:06,173
over a certain number of minutes
each day?

259
00:17:06,174 --> 00:17:07,720
No. Is there one?

260
00:17:08,944 --> 00:17:11,217
It gives no such warning.

261
00:17:58,964 --> 00:18:00,093
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

262
00:18:00,094 --> 00:18:01,294
˙Tis open.

263
00:18:08,574 --> 00:18:11,973
- Oh, I˙m sorry. I˙m interrupting.
- Just going over tomorrow˙s argument.

264
00:18:11,974 --> 00:18:14,143
Look, I shouldn˙t have come. I˙m...

265
00:18:14,144 --> 00:18:16,213
- Would you like some wine?
- No.

266
00:18:16,214 --> 00:18:20,653
- It doesn˙t mix with my medication.
- Or some tea?

267
00:18:20,654 --> 00:18:21,983
I˙ll clear a space.

268
00:18:21,984 --> 00:18:24,353
- I only moved in on Monday.
- Gosh.

269
00:18:24,354 --> 00:18:26,583
There˙s such a lot to do when you move.
You must be exhausted.

270
00:18:26,584 --> 00:18:29,393
Oh, I should˙ve moved years ago.

271
00:18:29,394 --> 00:18:34,863
You hang on, telling yourself it˙s for
the children. For their security.

272
00:18:34,864 --> 00:18:38,833
It was my husband˙s house.
His family owned it for ages.

273
00:18:38,834 --> 00:18:44,653
My life was so caught up with them,
then with work after he died.

274
00:18:44,874 --> 00:18:49,173
- I needed a big change.
- Look, I shouldn˙t have come.

275
00:18:49,174 --> 00:18:54,356
- Did you change your mind again?
- No. Nothing like that.

276
00:18:54,554 --> 00:18:56,282
I˙ll make some tea.

277
00:19:00,324 --> 00:19:02,093
Where are your children?

278
00:19:02,094 --> 00:19:06,123
Tom˙s at university.
Mark˙s out with his girlfriend.

279
00:19:06,124 --> 00:19:07,852
What about Michael?

280
00:19:08,464 --> 00:19:11,103
He˙s at a school friend˙s
playing computer games.

281
00:19:11,104 --> 00:19:14,073
It always ends badly
because Michael always wins.

282
00:19:14,074 --> 00:19:16,704
He˙s clever. He˙s fun, too.

283
00:19:18,144 --> 00:19:19,314
(SOBBING)

284
00:19:23,444 --> 00:19:24,464
Diana?

285
00:19:27,184 --> 00:19:29,457
- Is it the illness?
- No.

286
00:19:30,154 --> 00:19:34,523
I˙ve lived with illness as a nurse
and then a post-cancer counsellor.

287
00:19:34,524 --> 00:19:38,615
You just never think
it˙s gonna happen to you.

288
00:19:39,824 --> 00:19:41,114
It˙s not that.

289
00:19:42,494 --> 00:19:45,404
It˙s that in a year, maybe less,

290
00:19:45,734 --> 00:19:50,773
I won˙t be around and Michael will,
and I can˙t bear the thought of it.

291
00:19:50,774 --> 00:19:53,673
- Are the doctors that sure?
- That˙s what they all say.

292
00:19:53,674 --> 00:19:56,129
Well, it˙s only an opinion.

293
00:19:57,214 --> 00:20:02,253
Like the one I gave to your solicitor
and the Legal Services Commission.

294
00:20:02,254 --> 00:20:08,153
I weighed the available evidence
and I said that there was a 50%% chance

295
00:20:08,154 --> 00:20:11,393
that we would win.
There˙s a chance we˙ll lose, Diana,

296
00:20:11,394 --> 00:20:13,793
just as there˙s the chance
that you won˙t die.

297
00:20:13,794 --> 00:20:18,522
Doctors make pronouncements,
but anything can happen.

298
00:20:19,104 --> 00:20:21,733
When your children were growing up,

299
00:20:21,734 --> 00:20:23,703
did you ever think
that you might lose them?

300
00:20:23,704 --> 00:20:28,523
- That you might never see them again?
- All the time.

301
00:20:29,344 --> 00:20:30,604
(JO SIGHING)

302
00:20:32,244 --> 00:20:35,853
They were only young
when my husband fell ill.

303
00:20:35,854 --> 00:20:40,653
All my fears and anxieties
got projected onto the boys.

304
00:20:40,654 --> 00:20:43,123
At times I couldn˙t bear to let them
out of my sight.

305
00:20:43,124 --> 00:20:45,423
That˙s what I feel about Michael.

306
00:20:45,424 --> 00:20:47,793
Knowing that I won˙t be around,
I don˙t know how he˙ll cope

307
00:20:47,794 --> 00:20:50,463
- or who he˙ll turn to.
- Don˙t.

308
00:20:50,464 --> 00:20:54,464
Look, I have to say this
or I˙m gonna go mad.

309
00:20:54,574 --> 00:20:59,665
I˙ve been so selfish about Michael.
I wanted him so much.

310
00:21:00,744 --> 00:21:04,783
I got pregnant after a one-night stand
with a doctor at a party.

311
00:21:04,784 --> 00:21:09,253
I didn˙t even know his name.
I didn˙t want to after I got pregnant.

312
00:21:09,254 --> 00:21:13,436
I don˙t have any family.
Well, only my brother.

313
00:21:13,554 --> 00:21:15,645
Or any friends, really.

314
00:21:15,894 --> 00:21:20,733
I just poured everything into Michael
and now I˙m leaving him alone.

315
00:21:20,734 --> 00:21:22,553
You mustn˙t give up.

316
00:21:23,194 --> 00:21:25,194
Will you take him, Jo?

317
00:21:27,934 --> 00:21:30,333
What do you mean? Look after him?

318
00:21:30,334 --> 00:21:35,607
I know it˙s a lot to ask,
but I don˙t know what else to do.

319
00:21:37,374 --> 00:21:40,213
You don˙t know me. Well, not enough.

320
00:21:40,214 --> 00:21:45,023
I know you˙re a very caring person.
The way you helped my brother...

321
00:21:45,024 --> 00:21:46,983
And Michael really likes you.

322
00:21:46,984 --> 00:21:49,853
He says you˙re the most
interesting person he knows.

323
00:21:49,854 --> 00:21:51,423
You don˙t talk to him like a baby.

324
00:21:51,424 --> 00:21:55,393
- Children say all sorts of things.
- Yes, but he means it.

325
00:21:55,394 --> 00:22:00,133
He˙ll never cease to amaze you, Jo.
I promise you, he won˙t.

326
00:22:00,134 --> 00:22:04,498
He˙s so intelligent
and he˙s brilliantly behaved.

327
00:22:07,914 --> 00:22:12,243
He won˙t be any trouble. He won˙t.
He just likes to read all the time.

328
00:22:12,244 --> 00:22:15,453
But you don˙t know me at all.
You don˙t know if I˙m a good mother

329
00:22:15,454 --> 00:22:17,454
or reliable or coping.

330
00:22:17,854 --> 00:22:23,036
Do you know what it˙s like
to have to give your baby away?

331
00:22:24,024 --> 00:22:28,893
To entrust the most precious thing
in all the world to somebody else?

332
00:22:28,894 --> 00:22:30,349
I can˙t bear it.

333
00:22:31,234 --> 00:22:36,507
Just the thought that I won˙t be there
to help him grow up.

334
00:22:36,574 --> 00:22:39,343
To care for him when he˙s hurt.

335
00:22:39,344 --> 00:22:43,973
To tell him that everything˙s gonna
be all right when he˙s frightened.

336
00:22:43,974 --> 00:22:46,702
I don˙t want to leave him, Jo.

337
00:22:47,914 --> 00:22:51,551
I never thought I˙d have to. Not really.

338
00:22:52,584 --> 00:22:55,393
Somehow this all seems like a bad dream,

339
00:22:55,394 --> 00:22:59,293
that I˙m gonna wake up
and everything˙s gonna be all right.

340
00:22:59,294 --> 00:23:00,658
Well, it˙s not.

341
00:23:01,434 --> 00:23:06,162
And I just want to find
the right person for Michael.

342
00:23:09,034 --> 00:23:13,125
ON MACHINE: ThisisMark.
Staycool.Leaveanumber.

343
00:23:17,914 --> 00:23:20,543
ON MACHINE: Thisis JohnDeed.
Leavemeamessage, please.

344
00:23:20,544 --> 00:23:22,726
What can you do, anyway?

345
00:23:28,254 --> 00:23:30,453
Good evening, Mr Haughton, Mrs Channing.

346
00:23:30,454 --> 00:23:34,333
- We˙ll go straight through, Jules.
- Your guests are waiting.

347
00:23:34,334 --> 00:23:38,803
It˙s early days yet, Neil.
Don˙t let Tim Listfield rattle you.

348
00:23:38,804 --> 00:23:40,804
(MOBILE PHONE RINGING)

349
00:23:43,734 --> 00:23:47,734
You must know by now, George,
I don˙t rattle.

350
00:23:48,514 --> 00:23:52,060
Ah, I˙ll have a spritzer, Pepe. George?

351
00:23:52,584 --> 00:23:57,113
- A very large, very dry martini.
- Has it been one of those days?

352
00:23:57,114 --> 00:23:59,523
The sort I hoped
would never arrive, Max.

353
00:23:59,524 --> 00:24:03,993
Did you see what our shares did?
They dipped 11 points.

354
00:24:03,994 --> 00:24:08,133
- All the major shares dipped.
- I˙m only concerned about ours.

355
00:24:08,134 --> 00:24:11,703
Do you know what this means
for our capitalisation?

356
00:24:11,704 --> 00:24:16,633
We borrowed heavily to buy your
over-priced, third-generation licences.

357
00:24:16,634 --> 00:24:20,303
Well, as I remember, Tim,
you begged the DTI for them.

358
00:24:20,304 --> 00:24:24,383
- We didn˙t expect to pay 4 billion.
- You operate in a free market.

359
00:24:24,384 --> 00:24:30,983
- What we want now, Neil, are solutions.
- Well, Sir Ian Rochester is joining us.

360
00:24:30,984 --> 00:24:35,853
- Hopefully, he˙ll have some.
- Nothing˙s helped so far.

361
00:24:35,854 --> 00:24:39,582
I don˙t wish to indulge
in recriminations,

362
00:24:39,824 --> 00:24:43,393
but blocking Ms Hulsey˙s treatment
hurt us.

363
00:24:43,394 --> 00:24:45,304
Not my doing, George.

364
00:24:45,734 --> 00:24:48,694
Oh, as CEO I assumed it was.

365
00:24:50,774 --> 00:24:54,043
What we need is a strategy
to take Deed out of play.

366
00:24:54,044 --> 00:24:57,683
That˙s not the way
democratic government works.

367
00:24:57,684 --> 00:25:03,783
- We have a thing called separation of...
- I don˙t need a sermon, Neill

368
00:25:03,784 --> 00:25:08,623
Without systematic wealth creation,
democracy doesn˙t amount to smoke.

369
00:25:08,624 --> 00:25:11,993
Three hundred million has been wiped
off our shares.

370
00:25:11,994 --> 00:25:15,093
Sixty-five million off me personally.

371
00:25:15,094 --> 00:25:17,093
And all because you can˙t
handle a judge.

372
00:25:17,094 --> 00:25:18,903
Oh, we are going to handle him.

373
00:25:18,904 --> 00:25:23,233
- Then let˙s see the resultsl
- Why don˙t we all calm down?

374
00:25:23,234 --> 00:25:24,374
Thank you.

375
00:25:29,074 --> 00:25:30,529
Thank you, Pepe.

376
00:25:30,714 --> 00:25:33,204
Ah, Ian, good timing.

377
00:25:33,944 --> 00:25:36,634
- A drink?
- Um, a Tia, Pepe.

378
00:25:37,614 --> 00:25:39,223
- You know George.
- Yes.

379
00:25:39,224 --> 00:25:42,193
Max Solveigh,
marketing director of One-Way.

380
00:25:42,194 --> 00:25:44,474
Tim Listfield, his CEO.

381
00:25:44,994 --> 00:25:48,358
- Hello.
- The man with the solutions?

382
00:25:51,634 --> 00:25:56,180
- Is this the perennial problem?
- I hope not, Ian.

383
00:25:56,804 --> 00:26:00,077
We˙ve found the means to check Deed.

384
00:26:00,344 --> 00:26:05,013
- At the end of the hearing.
- When finally we˙re broke.

385
00:26:05,014 --> 00:26:06,883
NEIL: What is your end game, Ian?

386
00:26:06,884 --> 00:26:10,713
Because of his relationship
with the claimant˙s counsel,

387
00:26:10,714 --> 00:26:14,896
Deed should have recused himself
from the case.

388
00:26:14,994 --> 00:26:19,663
Their affair barely pours
since it started during our marriage.

389
00:26:19,664 --> 00:26:22,093
Deed denied any relationship
with Mrs Mills

390
00:26:22,094 --> 00:26:24,633
during her disciplinary tribunal.

391
00:26:24,634 --> 00:26:30,089
Yes. He˙s a High Court judge.
We were obliged to believe him.

392
00:26:31,004 --> 00:26:35,143
Whether he was sleeping with her
isn˙t important.

393
00:26:35,144 --> 00:26:38,413
His relationship with this woman
was extant

394
00:26:38,414 --> 00:26:41,913
through his appearing for her
as a defence witness.

395
00:26:41,914 --> 00:26:43,883
But is this enough
to get him overturned?

396
00:26:43,884 --> 00:26:49,339
Such subtle points exercise the minds
of appeal court judges.

397
00:26:50,254 --> 00:26:53,891
In the meantime,
our shares are hammered.

398
00:26:54,664 --> 00:26:57,937
And my company slips down the tubes.

399
00:27:04,004 --> 00:27:08,513
Do you know how I got to be
worth two billion personally?

400
00:27:08,514 --> 00:27:10,373
I bought some phones.

401
00:27:10,374 --> 00:27:13,783
I added value to them
and sold them for twice as much.

402
00:27:13,784 --> 00:27:18,923
I didn˙t buy toys for my children
with the profits or dresses for my wife.

403
00:27:18,924 --> 00:27:20,834
I bought more phones.

404
00:27:21,194 --> 00:27:23,393
And then I started
to manufacture phones.

405
00:27:23,394 --> 00:27:27,063
And then I bought rival companies.
And all the while,

406
00:27:27,064 --> 00:27:29,519
hanging on to what I˙d got.

407
00:27:31,034 --> 00:27:37,403
You think I˙m gonna let some little
nurse nobody take that away from me?

408
00:27:37,404 --> 00:27:40,041
I˙ll rip her heart out first.

409
00:27:43,644 --> 00:27:49,281
If you˙re not capable of neutering Deed,
I˙ll do it personally.

410
00:28:10,934 --> 00:28:12,025
(WHIMPERING)

411
00:28:13,904 --> 00:28:16,268
Someone fall off the roof?

412
00:28:16,514 --> 00:28:18,583
Can we get out of here?

413
00:28:18,584 --> 00:28:20,513
Mum says the building˙s bugged.

414
00:28:20,514 --> 00:28:25,083
She called me last night. She said I
shouldn˙t speak to you in the digs.

415
00:28:25,084 --> 00:28:26,254
(CHUCKLES)

416
00:28:26,954 --> 00:28:29,323
I am not going to get paranoid.

417
00:28:29,324 --> 00:28:30,923
She had dinner with her clients.

418
00:28:30,924 --> 00:28:33,593
She told me to warn you
to be on your guard.

419
00:28:33,594 --> 00:28:38,403
The CEO, Tim Listfield, is so mad
that the case is going on,

420
00:28:38,404 --> 00:28:40,677
he might try and get you.

421
00:28:42,274 --> 00:28:44,274
Well, did she say how?

422
00:28:45,474 --> 00:28:48,073
You have to be discreet.
Mum˙s in a really difficult position.

423
00:28:48,074 --> 00:28:50,873
If she has information that there˙s
been interference with the case,

424
00:28:50,874 --> 00:28:54,602
she must reveal it.
We˙ll have to abandon.

425
00:28:55,114 --> 00:28:59,123
No. No, no, no.
She wouldn˙t be that devious, would she?

426
00:28:59,124 --> 00:29:01,193
Dad, if this was
a trial-wrecking strategy,

427
00:29:01,194 --> 00:29:04,194
she˙d have to expose her clients.

428
00:29:05,624 --> 00:29:06,854
Yeah, maybe.

429
00:29:07,934 --> 00:29:12,063
Mrs Hulsey, let me offer you
the heartfelt sympathy

430
00:29:12,064 --> 00:29:15,003
of each of the directors
of One-Way Phones.

431
00:29:15,004 --> 00:29:18,273
Although they don˙t accept
their product is in any way responsible

432
00:29:18,274 --> 00:29:22,543
for your condition,
they hope the stress of all this won˙t

433
00:29:22,544 --> 00:29:24,817
aggravate your suffering.

434
00:29:27,284 --> 00:29:29,953
Are you well enough
to answer a few questions?

435
00:29:29,954 --> 00:29:31,004
Yes, I am.

436
00:29:31,224 --> 00:29:35,861
If you find any of this too difficult,
we will stop.

437
00:29:37,654 --> 00:29:41,363
Mrs Hulsey,
are either of your parents alive?

438
00:29:41,364 --> 00:29:42,593
No. Neither of them are.

439
00:29:42,594 --> 00:29:44,903
Could you tell the court
how they died, Mrs Hulsey?

440
00:29:44,904 --> 00:29:48,503
My Lord, my learned friend persists
on calling the claimant "Mrs".

441
00:29:48,504 --> 00:29:51,743
She knows from the reams
of documents that she is Ms.

442
00:29:51,744 --> 00:29:55,413
Yes. As a courtesy, you might use
the witness˙s correct title,

443
00:29:55,414 --> 00:29:56,687
Miss Channing.

444
00:29:59,314 --> 00:30:01,713
I do beg your pardon, Ms Hulsey.

445
00:30:01,714 --> 00:30:05,533
- You are an unmarried mother.
- Yes, I am.

446
00:30:05,984 --> 00:30:09,223
This situation must cause you
added stress.

447
00:30:09,224 --> 00:30:11,588
No. I chose it for myself.

448
00:30:12,464 --> 00:30:14,633
You don˙t find not having
a partner˙s support,

449
00:30:14,634 --> 00:30:18,103
making all the decisions yourself,
stressful?

450
00:30:18,104 --> 00:30:21,503
Yes. Sometimes I wish the father
of Michael were around.

451
00:30:21,504 --> 00:30:24,643
You must worry about Michael˙s safety.

452
00:30:24,644 --> 00:30:27,008
Of course. I˙m his mother.

453
00:30:29,044 --> 00:30:30,408
(CLEARS THROAT)

454
00:30:31,744 --> 00:30:34,713
You were going to tell me
about the death of your parents.

455
00:30:34,714 --> 00:30:37,260
What was the cause of death?

456
00:30:38,024 --> 00:30:41,752
They both died of cancer.
Two years apart.

457
00:30:42,524 --> 00:30:46,161
My mother died of breast cancer in 1993.

458
00:30:47,024 --> 00:30:49,833
And two years later,
my father died of a brain tumour.

459
00:30:49,834 --> 00:30:52,563
Did anyone else in your family
die of cancer?

460
00:30:52,564 --> 00:30:56,273
I don˙t know.
I don˙t really know my extended family.

461
00:30:56,274 --> 00:30:57,547
If I have one.

462
00:30:57,634 --> 00:31:01,764
Then possibly all of them
died of cancer.

463
00:31:11,984 --> 00:31:13,154
Ms Hulsey?

464
00:31:15,224 --> 00:31:19,423
- What was the question?
- Do you need to take a break?

465
00:31:19,424 --> 00:31:22,233
- What was the question?
- Would you like me to stop?

466
00:31:22,234 --> 00:31:24,416
No. I˙m fine, thank you.

467
00:31:24,434 --> 00:31:28,344
How well do you cope with stress,
Ms Hulsey?

468
00:31:28,434 --> 00:31:30,707
Depends on the situation.

469
00:31:31,034 --> 00:31:34,671
Did you scream at an assistant
in Tesco˙s

470
00:31:34,844 --> 00:31:37,273
when they ran out of Rice Krispies?

471
00:31:37,274 --> 00:31:40,243
Become so angry
the police had to be called?

472
00:31:40,244 --> 00:31:45,517
It wasn˙t just the Rice Krispies.
It had been a hectic day.

473
00:31:45,524 --> 00:31:47,353
I˙d had a headache for two days.

474
00:31:47,354 --> 00:31:50,493
The assistant being rude
was just the last straw.

475
00:31:50,494 --> 00:31:55,585
I˙m sure everyone on the jury
has had those sort of days.

476
00:31:55,964 --> 00:31:59,601
It˙s how we cope with them that matters.

477
00:32:00,234 --> 00:32:02,344
Look at item 33B.

478
00:32:02,704 --> 00:32:05,443
Your annual assessment
from your supervisor,

479
00:32:05,444 --> 00:32:07,803
the year before you left the hospital.

480
00:32:07,804 --> 00:32:11,943
"Dedicated to the work,
but needs to learn to relax.

481
00:32:11,944 --> 00:32:16,053
"We fear she˙ll make herself ill and
totally alienate friends and colleagues

482
00:32:16,054 --> 00:32:19,983
"unless she learns to cope
with everyday stresses."

483
00:32:19,984 --> 00:32:21,823
She had no right to judge me.

484
00:32:21,824 --> 00:32:25,393
Isn˙t it true
that you have alienated people?

485
00:32:25,394 --> 00:32:30,333
Well, some people find it hard
to deal with the terminally ill.

486
00:32:30,334 --> 00:32:35,003
Ms Hulsey, do you know the theory
Brain Tumour Trauma?

487
00:32:35,004 --> 00:32:36,273
Yes. I˙ve heard of it.

488
00:32:36,274 --> 00:32:40,803
Can you recall a fairly recent
major trauma in your life?

489
00:32:40,804 --> 00:32:43,213
Are you offering evidence
to support this argument?

490
00:32:43,214 --> 00:32:45,113
I will be exploring the hypothesis.

491
00:32:45,114 --> 00:32:49,383
Well, as long as the jury understands,
this is only a hypothesis.

492
00:32:49,384 --> 00:32:51,653
Ms Hulsey, I will repeat the question.

493
00:32:51,654 --> 00:32:56,109
Can you recall
a fairly major trauma in your life?

494
00:32:56,354 --> 00:32:57,523
No, not really.

495
00:32:57,524 --> 00:33:01,263
What˙s the biggest scare you˙ve had
in the last two and a half years?

496
00:33:01,264 --> 00:33:04,833
Once when Michael was out alone
for most of the night.

497
00:33:04,834 --> 00:33:10,016
I thought the parent of a school friend
had picked him up.

498
00:33:10,374 --> 00:33:11,544
She hadn˙t.

499
00:33:12,774 --> 00:33:15,502
He couldn˙t find his way home.

500
00:33:16,114 --> 00:33:18,843
It was 2:00 in the morning
before the police found him.

501
00:33:18,844 --> 00:33:21,208
- It was terrifying.
- Yes.

502
00:33:21,714 --> 00:33:23,351
It must have been.

503
00:33:23,754 --> 00:33:26,583
Did you scream at the policewoman,
"If anything has happened to him,

504
00:33:26,584 --> 00:33:29,323
- "I˙ll kill myself"?
- Well, I would have done.

505
00:33:29,324 --> 00:33:32,961
I never let him
out of my sight any more.

506
00:33:33,924 --> 00:33:37,063
Added to the stress
you were already suffering,

507
00:33:37,064 --> 00:33:41,454
plus a possible family predisposition
to cancer,

508
00:33:43,404 --> 00:33:47,041
mightn˙t this be the cause
of your tumour

509
00:33:47,074 --> 00:33:49,802
rather than your mobile phone?

510
00:33:53,244 --> 00:33:55,154
Thank you, Ms Hulsey.

511
00:33:58,084 --> 00:34:01,523
Mrs Mills,
we could adjourn a little early.

512
00:34:01,524 --> 00:34:03,615
I˙ll be brief, My Lord.

513
00:34:05,764 --> 00:34:08,037
- Can you continue?
- Yes.

514
00:34:11,864 --> 00:34:14,774
Ms Hulsey, can you tell the jury

515
00:34:16,834 --> 00:34:19,289
what your son means to you?

516
00:34:20,274 --> 00:34:23,911
What leaving him will mean when you die?

517
00:34:28,514 --> 00:34:30,333
I can˙t describe it.

518
00:34:33,924 --> 00:34:39,015
Sometimes I sit with him
on his bed till he falls asleep.

519
00:34:41,364 --> 00:34:45,603
Sometimes I sit there for hours
just watching him breathing.

520
00:34:45,604 --> 00:34:49,604
He˙s the best thing
that ever happened to me.

521
00:34:50,974 --> 00:34:54,156
And soon I˙ll be leaving him alone.

522
00:34:55,174 --> 00:35:00,265
I don˙t know that...
If he understands that it˙s forever.

523
00:35:04,984 --> 00:35:07,075
What I˙m most afraid of

524
00:35:11,354 --> 00:35:13,900
is that I might die suddenly

525
00:35:14,624 --> 00:35:18,988
and I won˙t be able to say,
"Goodbye, my darling.

526
00:35:20,204 --> 00:35:22,932
"Mum will always be with you."

527
00:35:47,564 --> 00:35:50,474
Why did you keep our Beanobooks?

528
00:35:54,734 --> 00:35:58,073
- And our three-legged dog stories?
- Tom used to read them to you.

529
00:35:58,074 --> 00:35:59,373
Well, he won˙t be reading them any more.

530
00:35:59,374 --> 00:36:00,673
Your children might.

531
00:36:00,674 --> 00:36:02,813
It˙s the past, Mum.
It clutters up your life.

532
00:36:02,814 --> 00:36:05,013
Everything is the past, Mark.

533
00:36:05,014 --> 00:36:09,105
- It isn˙t clutter.
- Isn˙t that why we moved?

534
00:36:09,314 --> 00:36:11,453
- What if we didn˙t have books?
- There˙s the internet.

535
00:36:11,454 --> 00:36:15,364
- It˙s not the same.
- It˙s more up-to-date.

536
00:36:17,524 --> 00:36:19,423
Did you think
about what Diana Hulsey asked?

537
00:36:19,424 --> 00:36:23,334
I think you˙d be mad
to even contemplate it.

538
00:36:24,034 --> 00:36:26,762
(SIGHING) It˙s up to you, Mum.

539
00:36:26,964 --> 00:36:31,273
- I˙ll be away at university anyway.
- It˙s still your home and Tom˙s.

540
00:36:31,274 --> 00:36:34,373
I can˙t believe you˙re seriously
thinking about lumbering yourself

541
00:36:34,374 --> 00:36:38,465
with another kid.
I mean, could you even cope?

542
00:36:39,014 --> 00:36:40,651
No, I suppose not.

543
00:36:42,914 --> 00:36:46,153
I keep thinking about that boy
ending up on his own.

544
00:36:46,154 --> 00:36:48,523
His mother doesn˙t seem
to have anyone else to turn to.

545
00:36:48,524 --> 00:36:49,723
Not even any friends.

546
00:36:49,724 --> 00:36:51,893
Well, these people always seem
to find you, don˙t they, Mum?

547
00:36:51,894 --> 00:36:54,223
- You˙re a soft touch.
- Am I?

548
00:36:54,224 --> 00:36:57,163
- I said no.
- Most people would just forget about it.

549
00:36:57,164 --> 00:37:00,133
It may never happen.
Doctors often get these things wrong.

550
00:37:00,134 --> 00:37:03,603
What if they get it entirely wrong
and she dies tomorrow?

551
00:37:03,604 --> 00:37:07,003
I thought about giving up you and Tom
after your dad died.

552
00:37:07,004 --> 00:37:10,173
I couldn˙t cope.
I was too depressed and exhausted.

553
00:37:10,174 --> 00:37:15,811
I asked your grandma to take you
and do you know what she said?

554
00:37:15,954 --> 00:37:16,954
Mum?

555
00:37:17,254 --> 00:37:21,436
Can you even imagine
how Diana must be feeling?

556
00:37:21,454 --> 00:37:23,324
No, not really.

557
00:37:33,004 --> 00:37:36,203
We must be grateful
for some consistency.

558
00:37:36,204 --> 00:37:41,003
I˙ve pondered long and hard
over your hypothetical, Ian.

559
00:37:41,004 --> 00:37:43,413
I assume it was hypothetical.

560
00:37:43,414 --> 00:37:47,043
At the moment. It may become fact
and come to the court of appeal.

561
00:37:47,044 --> 00:37:48,913
Ah, then perhaps
I shouldn˙t become involved.

562
00:37:48,914 --> 00:37:52,653
- It may come to me.
- We˙d make sure it didn˙t.

563
00:37:52,654 --> 00:37:54,927
- Hello, Jerry.
- Morning.

564
00:37:56,454 --> 00:38:00,993
I˙ve taken the minds of Sean Arnold
and Matthew Waugh on the subject.

565
00:38:00,994 --> 00:38:04,133
I˙m intrigued, Joe.
Left, right and centre.

566
00:38:04,134 --> 00:38:05,771
We were unanimous.

567
00:38:05,904 --> 00:38:08,503
If a judge has a relationship
with a barrister,

568
00:38:08,504 --> 00:38:12,173
being a witness of both fact
and character for him,

569
00:38:12,174 --> 00:38:15,243
and subsequently has that barrister
before him in a case,

570
00:38:15,244 --> 00:38:19,413
he must recuse himself
or run the risk of being overturned.

571
00:38:19,414 --> 00:38:21,414
- Excellent.
- Er, Ian?

572
00:38:24,354 --> 00:38:27,424
Sean thought it might be Deed.

573
00:38:30,394 --> 00:38:33,723
Monty Everard was
complaining about him in the club.

574
00:38:33,724 --> 00:38:34,774
Was he?

575
00:38:39,034 --> 00:38:40,204
Dr Colbin,

576
00:38:41,034 --> 00:38:45,003
when you were forced to abandon
Ms Hulsey˙s experimental therapy,

577
00:38:45,004 --> 00:38:47,443
did you tell a Sergeant Bridges

578
00:38:47,444 --> 00:38:51,643
that the reason was that One-Way Phones
had leaned on the hospital lawyers?

579
00:38:51,644 --> 00:38:54,513
My Lord, my learned friend
is seeking to introduce this

580
00:38:54,514 --> 00:38:57,913
- as if it were a statement of fact.
- I˙m merely asking a question.

581
00:38:57,914 --> 00:38:59,653
To besmirch my clients.

582
00:38:59,654 --> 00:39:03,893
Address the court, not each other.
You should know better, both of you.

583
00:39:03,894 --> 00:39:07,193
I˙m sure Mrs Mills is trying to
do just that.

584
00:39:07,194 --> 00:39:09,558
But it˙s a valid question.

585
00:39:11,494 --> 00:39:12,858
(CLEARS THROAT)

586
00:39:13,304 --> 00:39:17,395
I did say that,
but I˙m not sure if it˙s true.

587
00:39:22,874 --> 00:39:24,329
Then why say it?

588
00:39:25,114 --> 00:39:30,387
Well, because I was cross
when I had to stop the treatment.

589
00:39:32,284 --> 00:39:33,853
Have you been leaned on, too?

590
00:39:33,854 --> 00:39:35,893
- My Lord...
- I˙m sure the jury is curious

591
00:39:35,894 --> 00:39:39,531
as to why the doctor˙s changed his mind.

592
00:39:39,594 --> 00:39:42,140
Answer the question, Doctor.

593
00:39:42,894 --> 00:39:46,163
(STAMMERING) When my anger subsided,

594
00:39:46,164 --> 00:39:50,803
I realised that I had been a little rash
when Sergeant Bridges spoke to me.

595
00:39:50,804 --> 00:39:53,273
In other words,
your job has been made more secure

596
00:39:53,274 --> 00:39:57,002
by a research programme
funded by One-Way.

597
00:39:57,344 --> 00:40:01,083
The hospital is conducting research
funded by One-Way?

598
00:40:01,084 --> 00:40:02,683
That has no bearing.

599
00:40:02,684 --> 00:40:06,123
You didn˙t inform the claimant˙s
legal team about your change of mind?

600
00:40:06,124 --> 00:40:07,488
(PHONE RINGING)

601
00:40:07,684 --> 00:40:10,594
No, sir. I didn˙t know I had to.

602
00:40:11,124 --> 00:40:14,579
I was asked to discuss Diana˙s tumour.

603
00:40:15,964 --> 00:40:20,303
Do you wish to treat this witness
as hostile, Mrs Mills?

604
00:40:20,304 --> 00:40:21,414
I do not.

605
00:40:27,604 --> 00:40:31,413
Dr Colbin, is the brain tumour
Ms Hulsey is suffering from common?

606
00:40:31,414 --> 00:40:34,505
No, but it˙s becoming more common.

607
00:40:35,454 --> 00:40:39,723
A GP who would have expected to see
one or two over an entire career

608
00:40:39,724 --> 00:40:41,423
is now seeing two a year.

609
00:40:41,424 --> 00:40:43,493
How long has this incidence been rising?

610
00:40:43,494 --> 00:40:44,553
Over the last decade.

611
00:40:44,554 --> 00:40:46,693
Since the widespread use
of mobile phones.

612
00:40:46,694 --> 00:40:48,693
My Lord, my learned friend
is leading the witness.

613
00:40:48,694 --> 00:40:50,513
Yes, yes. Mrs Mills.

614
00:40:52,964 --> 00:40:55,419
Dr Colbin, in your opinion,

615
00:40:56,574 --> 00:41:00,943
is there any connection between
the rise in incidence of tumours

616
00:41:00,944 --> 00:41:04,673
in this part of the brain
and the use of mobile phones?

617
00:41:04,674 --> 00:41:06,947
There˙s no hard evidence.

618
00:41:10,654 --> 00:41:14,291
Armac, I can˙t believe
you just did that.

619
00:41:15,024 --> 00:41:16,753
Why did you do that?
You˙ve ruined our case.

620
00:41:16,754 --> 00:41:18,353
I˙m sorry, Di.
I just can˙t talk at the moment.

621
00:41:18,354 --> 00:41:20,363
They stopped my treatment
and you know why.

622
00:41:20,364 --> 00:41:21,933
You should take their offer.

623
00:41:21,934 --> 00:41:24,793
- You still can.
- You know I can˙t do that.

624
00:41:24,794 --> 00:41:29,067
Not after what they˙ve done.
You˙ve helped them.

625
00:41:29,174 --> 00:41:31,973
I trusted you.
I thought you were my friend.

626
00:41:31,974 --> 00:41:33,873
The stress of all this
isn˙t helping your chances.

627
00:41:33,874 --> 00:41:39,483
What you˙ve done is taken away
any chance that Michael ever had.

628
00:41:39,484 --> 00:41:42,848
Now he˙ll be left alone with nothing.

629
00:41:52,464 --> 00:41:56,393
Take the book in your hand
and read the card, please.

630
00:41:56,394 --> 00:41:58,264
(SCOFFING) "I swear

631
00:41:58,864 --> 00:42:02,333
"that the evidence I shall give
shall be the truth

632
00:42:02,334 --> 00:42:05,971
"and nothing but the truth
as I know it."

633
00:42:06,904 --> 00:42:09,173
You find the oath inadequate, Professor?

634
00:42:09,174 --> 00:42:11,743
I can only speak the truth
as I interpret it.

635
00:42:11,744 --> 00:42:15,381
It is neither the truth, the whole truth

636
00:42:15,514 --> 00:42:17,878
nor nothing but the truth.

637
00:42:18,854 --> 00:42:20,883
I appreciate your candour.

638
00:42:20,884 --> 00:42:22,248
(ALL CHUCKLING)

639
00:42:23,124 --> 00:42:26,463
Professor, are you the head
of molecular neurology

640
00:42:26,464 --> 00:42:29,463
- at Oxford University?
- You know I am.

641
00:42:29,464 --> 00:42:31,646
Yes. It˙s for the court.

642
00:42:33,504 --> 00:42:37,533
Have you become the foremost expert
on astrocytomas,

643
00:42:37,534 --> 00:42:39,503
the type of brain tumour
Diana Hulsey has?

644
00:42:39,504 --> 00:42:42,773
Well, that label has been
stuck on me, unfortunately.

645
00:42:42,774 --> 00:42:46,483
Is this false modesty or are you indeed
highly qualified, Professor,

646
00:42:46,484 --> 00:42:49,303
because the jury needs to know.

647
00:42:49,714 --> 00:42:53,533
Let us say I know a lot
about such tumours.

648
00:42:54,624 --> 00:42:59,897
Then we˙ll accept you
as the foremost expert in your field.

649
00:43:01,064 --> 00:43:03,163
As such, can you tell us

650
00:43:03,164 --> 00:43:06,563
why this sort of tumour
is on the increase, Professor?

651
00:43:06,564 --> 00:43:11,103
Even with my grand label,
I can only give an opinion.

652
00:43:11,104 --> 00:43:15,743
In my opinion, it˙s due to the frequent
and persistent

653
00:43:15,744 --> 00:43:20,083
interruption of molecular activity
in the brain by microwaves.

654
00:43:20,084 --> 00:43:23,453
Do we know what˙s brought about
this increase?

655
00:43:23,454 --> 00:43:26,253
The increased use of television.

656
00:43:26,254 --> 00:43:28,709
Microwave ovens, computers.

657
00:43:28,894 --> 00:43:32,163
The single biggest cause
is mobile telephones

658
00:43:32,164 --> 00:43:35,255
used against the side of the head.

659
00:43:35,434 --> 00:43:39,163
They create heating. They interrupt
the molecular connections.

660
00:43:39,164 --> 00:43:43,603
They heat, in particular,
the cortical surface of the brain.

661
00:43:43,604 --> 00:43:47,043
Due to the angle
which the phone is held at,

662
00:43:47,044 --> 00:43:50,643
most microwaves are directed
to the parietal lobe.

663
00:43:50,644 --> 00:43:54,443
- Is there any way to avoid this?
- Yes. Don˙t use mobile phones.

664
00:43:54,444 --> 00:43:55,754
(ALL LAUGHING)

665
00:43:56,914 --> 00:44:01,223
How long does the sort of tumour
that Ms Hulsey has take to develop?

666
00:44:01,224 --> 00:44:04,134
The time is infinitely variable.

667
00:44:04,324 --> 00:44:08,093
These tests haven˙t been done
in humans, only animals.

668
00:44:08,094 --> 00:44:12,403
But brain tumours are
the fastest growing cancers.

669
00:44:12,404 --> 00:44:16,473
Three to six months
from the breakdown of cells

670
00:44:16,474 --> 00:44:19,020
to detection would be usual.

671
00:44:19,104 --> 00:44:24,741
- Did you examine Ms Hulsey?
- You know I did. You asked me to.

672
00:44:25,814 --> 00:44:30,283
Did you draw any conclusion
as to the cause of her tumour?

673
00:44:30,284 --> 00:44:31,684
In my opinion,

674
00:44:33,284 --> 00:44:36,953
the persistent use of her mobile phone
was the cause.

675
00:44:36,954 --> 00:44:38,318
(ALL MURMURING)

676
00:44:39,764 --> 00:44:43,993
How did you come to your conclusion
without putting humans at risk?

677
00:44:43,994 --> 00:44:48,133
Humans are quite able
to put themselves at risk.

678
00:44:48,134 --> 00:44:50,794
We use mice and extrapolate.

679
00:44:51,934 --> 00:44:53,389
You surprise me.

680
00:44:54,044 --> 00:44:56,863
Is a mouse the same as a human?

681
00:44:56,874 --> 00:44:58,813
I˙ve never seen one
using a mobile phone.

682
00:44:58,814 --> 00:45:02,451
Well, you should come to our laboratory.

683
00:45:03,584 --> 00:45:07,221
Exposed to pulsed
900 megahertz radiation

684
00:45:07,654 --> 00:45:09,693
for one hour a day for three months,

685
00:45:09,694 --> 00:45:14,422
mice show a significant doubling
of B-cell lymphomas.

686
00:45:14,724 --> 00:45:16,793
Animal rights activists

687
00:45:16,794 --> 00:45:20,763
claim that animals are so
significantly different from humans

688
00:45:20,764 --> 00:45:24,233
as to render
the whole work unscientific.

689
00:45:24,234 --> 00:45:28,507
Mmm. That is true of many
pharmaceutical trials.

690
00:45:28,874 --> 00:45:30,074
Animals...

691
00:45:30,874 --> 00:45:34,383
can be quite different from humans
in their responses.

692
00:45:34,384 --> 00:45:36,483
Is much of this work inaccurate?

693
00:45:36,484 --> 00:45:40,121
Most scientific work is
subject to error.

694
00:45:41,084 --> 00:45:46,175
Could you have made an error
about Diana Hulsey˙s tumour?

695
00:45:48,394 --> 00:45:49,684
It˙s possible.

696
00:45:49,934 --> 00:45:55,025
So, what we have here is
a biased and error-prone opinion

697
00:45:55,774 --> 00:45:57,865
extrapolated from mice.

698
00:45:58,774 --> 00:46:00,034
(SNICKERING)

699
